cartridge "SPACE GALLERY/COMPUTE GAZETTE/1985"

' SPACE GALLERY
' A ColecoVision game based on "Space Gallery", COMPUTE!'s Gazette, July
' 1985 p. 46, by Jeff A. Lapkoff (Commodore 64 machine language, SYS2062). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by
' Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a byte-for-byte port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/sgall.dis, timed in jsc64 with orig/time-sgall.mjs and
' orig/bomb-sgall.mjs), rebuilt for the ColecoVision with new aliens, a
' starry sky, sprites, sounds and a title tune.
'
' The game (one player): a laser cannon slides along a launch deck at the
' bottom. Four aliens ("frisboids" in the article; their bombs are
' "pellets") fly the same looping path, one behind the other, and drop
' pellets (at most three at a time). Shoot all four to clear the wave, 5
' points each; five waves make a level, then a SHOT BONUS of 5 points for
' each shot under 60 fired in that level. A bomb on the cannon costs one of the
' five cannons and starts the wave again with all four aliens on a new
' path. The game is over when the last cannon is lost.
'
' From the original (orig/sgall.dis): the 16 flight paths (the C64's
' tables, mapped to this screen; the aliens cycle through the first
' (n - 1) / 2 waypoints, starting together at one point and fanning out to
' waypoints 1-4); every leg of the path takes the same time (256 passes,
' 31-33 jiffies measured: 32 frames here), so long legs are fast; a new
' random path every wave and after every lost cannon; up to eleven shots in
' the air, one row (8 pixels) a frame; a press fires at once, holding fires
' again every 24 frames (200 passes); a shot flies on through what it hits;
' 5 points an alien; 3 bomb places; only the second, third and fourth alien
' drop bombs (the first never does), very often when right above the cannon
' (6 in 128 a pass) and now and then anywhere, so the three bombs are
' almost always falling; bombs fall about 2 C64 pixels a frame (here 5
' pixels in 3 frames over a shorter sky, the same time); the cannon moves
' about 2.8 pixels a frame (here 8 in 3 frames); SHOT BONUS (60 - shots) x
' 5 (shots counted up to 60) after five waves; LEVEL shown between waves; one extra cannon
' on reaching level 5; HEAT SEEKER (an option on the title, off at the
' start): from level 4 the third bomb steers toward the cannon as fast as
' it falls, drawn blue as in the article; a high score.
'
' Liberties: shots are bolt tiles in four colours (the C64 drew a random
' line character); hits are tested against each alien's box instead of
' the C64's sprite-to-character collision; an alien's explosion lasts 16
' frames (the C64: about 7) and scores at once; a smaller playfield (the
' C64's sky was wider and taller; the paths are scaled to it); aliens and
' bombs stay inside the screen (the C64 let them run into the border); the
' first alien, which never bombs on the C64, bombs when it is the last one
' left (on the C64 a player could leave it flying and never be bombed); the
' C64's "PRESS UP TO PLAY AGAIN / DOWN TO END" is replaced by the house
' ending (a press goes back to the title); new aliens, cannon, deck and
' starry sky; sounds and a new title tune; a credits page.
'
' Controls: joystick only (either controller): left/right move the
' cannon, either button fires (hold to keep firing). On the title,
' up/down choose PLAY / HEAT SEEKER / CREDITS, a button confirms (on HEAT
' SEEKER it switches the option on or off).
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` after every `clear
' sprites`. Own xorshift random numbers.

